1
00:00:12,534 --> 00:00:14,492
[cell phone rings]

2
00:00:14,579 --> 00:00:15,624
Morning, Lisbon.

3
00:00:15,711 --> 00:00:17,800
Morning. You close?

4
00:00:17,887 --> 00:00:20,150
No. Flat tire.

5
00:00:20,237 --> 00:00:22,022
Alright. We'll talk
when you get here.

6
00:00:22,109 --> 00:00:25,199
Wait, just 'cause I'm not there
doesn't mean I can't help.

7
00:00:25,286 --> 00:00:27,244
Hold on a sec.
What do you got?

8
00:00:27,331 --> 00:00:28,550
Victim's name is
Michelle Karp...

9
00:00:28,637 --> 00:00:30,247
I'm putting you on speaker.

10
00:00:30,334 --> 00:00:32,249
'Nineteen. She left
the restaurant where she worked'

11
00:00:32,336 --> 00:00:34,556
'around 11:00 p.m.
Never made it home.'

12
00:00:34,643 --> 00:00:35,861
She was abducted and murdered.

13
00:00:35,948 --> 00:00:37,733
Her body was dumped
here last night.

14
00:00:37,820 --> 00:00:39,256
Walker found her
an hour ago.

15
00:00:39,343 --> 00:00:40,866
- Thanks.
- Alright.

16
00:00:40,953 --> 00:00:43,043
[sighs]
The body's
on state park land

17
00:00:43,130 --> 00:00:45,697
near a picnic area
not far from the road.

18
00:00:45,784 --> 00:00:47,264
How was she killed?

19
00:00:47,351 --> 00:00:50,311
Her throat was slit.
It looks pretty clean, precise.

20
00:00:50,398 --> 00:00:52,530
- Mm. Blood?
- 'Hardly a drop.'

21
00:00:52,617 --> 00:00:55,229
She's fully clothed,
so probably no sexual assault.

22
00:00:55,316 --> 00:00:58,449
'Her hands and feet
are bound with wire.'

23
00:00:58,536 --> 00:01:00,495
Small pebbles have been
placed on her eyes.

24
00:01:00,582 --> 00:01:03,759
Huh, nice touch.
Pebbles from the crime scene?

25
00:01:03,846 --> 00:01:05,413
It looks like it, yeah.

26
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:08,764
And the...oh. And the wire?

27
00:01:08,851 --> 00:01:11,723
It's knotted.
Neatly, carefully.

28
00:01:11,810 --> 00:01:14,117
Someone took their time.

29
00:01:16,902 --> 00:01:18,513
Yeah, you're right.

30
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:20,471
I didn't say anything.

31
00:01:20,558 --> 00:01:22,995
No, but you were thinking
whoever did this

32
00:01:23,083 --> 00:01:25,824
has killed before
many times.

33
00:01:25,911 --> 00:01:27,870
Yes, but I can't
assume that.

34
00:01:27,957 --> 00:01:29,567
Trust your instincts,
Lisbon.

35
00:01:29,654 --> 00:01:33,484
Boss. Just got off the phone
with Fresno P.D. The killer's

36
00:01:33,571 --> 00:01:36,531
'M.O. fits four other murders
they've had in 18 months.'

37
00:01:36,618 --> 00:01:38,359
Looks like we've got
a serial killer.

38
00:01:40,274 --> 00:01:42,189
'Huh? See?'

39
00:01:42,276 --> 00:01:44,147
You called it, Lisbon.

40
00:01:44,234 --> 00:01:46,628
You called it!

41
00:01:48,369 --> 00:01:51,241
[theme music]

42
00:02:04,211 --> 00:02:06,648
Fresno P.D. sent over
their files an hour ago.

43
00:02:06,735 --> 00:02:08,215
Here's what we got so far.

44
00:02:08,302 --> 00:02:10,217
The San Joaquin killer
abducts young girls.

45
00:02:10,304 --> 00:02:12,567
All between the ages
of 16 and 20.

46
00:02:12,654 --> 00:02:15,352
He takes them to a location
where he binds their hands

47
00:02:15,439 --> 00:02:17,920
and feet with wire, cuts
their throat and dumps them.

48
00:02:18,007 --> 00:02:20,923
Objects found near the crime
scene are placed over the eyes.

49
00:02:21,010 --> 00:02:22,968
Pebbles, glass pieces,
and bottle caps so far.

50
00:02:23,055 --> 00:02:24,970
Any pattern to where
the bodies were found?

51
00:02:25,057 --> 00:02:26,668
It seems random, but --

52
00:02:26,755 --> 00:02:29,410
Fresno P.D. said the girls
were taken at opportune moments.

53
00:02:29,497 --> 00:02:31,194
Often the only time
they were alone.

54
00:02:31,281 --> 00:02:32,630
Meaning he stalks them first.

55
00:02:32,717 --> 00:02:34,458
Does Fresno P.D.
have a suspect?

56
00:02:34,545 --> 00:02:37,635
Yep.

57
00:02:37,722 --> 00:02:39,420
- Eight suspects?
- 'Yeah.'

58
00:02:39,507 --> 00:02:42,510
Due to the lack of evidence, the
list hasn't been narrowed down.

59
00:02:42,597 --> 00:02:45,382
Grab Cho, get the list of names,
and talk to them.

60
00:02:45,469 --> 00:02:47,906
- Meh.
- What?

61
00:02:47,993 --> 00:02:49,560
You're just going
through the motions.

62
00:02:49,647 --> 00:02:50,909
Yeah, it's called
police work.

63
00:02:50,996 --> 00:02:52,824
Well, why not try
a fresh approach?

64
00:02:52,911 --> 00:02:54,304
Like what, tarot cards?

65
00:02:54,391 --> 00:02:56,698
Go directly to the one
that feels right.

66
00:02:56,785 --> 00:02:58,787
Look, these are all
viable suspects.

67
00:02:58,874 --> 00:03:00,832
The Fresno P.D. did
their job well.

68
00:03:00,919 --> 00:03:03,270
So you're gonna do
the same job over again?

69
00:03:03,357 --> 00:03:06,229
Take a close look at these guys.
Use your intuition.

70
00:03:06,316 --> 00:03:07,709
Which one is guilty?

71
00:03:09,363 --> 00:03:10,842
- I don't know.
- Oh, come on.

72
00:03:10,929 --> 00:03:12,801
You've been
a cop for a while.

73
00:03:12,888 --> 00:03:15,369
What you don't know is what you
know without knowing.

74
00:03:15,456 --> 00:03:19,068
- Take a guess and run with it.
- Alright. Okay.

75
00:03:19,155 --> 00:03:23,812
- Okay.
- Alright. Okay.

76
00:03:23,899 --> 00:03:26,423
Not him.

77
00:03:26,510 --> 00:03:28,425
Bow tie, no.

78
00:03:32,603 --> 00:03:33,822
Not him.

79
00:03:33,909 --> 00:03:35,824
[photo rustles]

80
00:03:35,911 --> 00:03:37,260
Or him.

81
00:03:37,347 --> 00:03:39,219
[photo rustles]

82
00:03:40,394 --> 00:03:42,744
It could be him.

83
00:03:42,831 --> 00:03:44,876
Sneaky eyes.

84
00:03:46,617 --> 00:03:48,837
On the other hand,
he's got a college education.

85
00:03:48,924 --> 00:03:50,491
That profiling blather,
forget about that.

86
00:03:50,578 --> 00:03:51,666
Go with your gut.

87
00:03:51,753 --> 00:03:54,712
[sighs]

88
00:03:54,799 --> 00:03:57,672
There. In seconds,
you've whittled it down

89
00:03:57,759 --> 00:03:59,717
to a manageable
three suspects.

90
00:03:59,804 --> 00:04:02,024
Which one of these guys
jumps out at you?

91
00:04:03,678 --> 00:04:06,463
[telephone rings in distance]

92
00:04:09,161 --> 00:04:11,816
This one?

93
00:04:11,903 --> 00:04:14,471
Yeah, but it's just a hunch.
I don't know why.

94
00:04:14,558 --> 00:04:18,214
Best kind of hunch.
As good a place to start as any.

95
00:04:19,389 --> 00:04:21,086
Nicely done, Lisbon.

96
00:04:21,173 --> 00:04:23,437
[Van Pelt]
'His name is Richard Haibach.'

97
00:04:23,524 --> 00:04:25,830
'He's 44 years old,
lives alone.'

98
00:04:25,917 --> 00:04:29,181
He has an alibi for two murders,
but Fresno P.D. logged

99
00:04:29,269 --> 00:04:31,227
complaints against him
for loitering.

100
00:04:31,314 --> 00:04:33,621
Apparently he likes to park
outside local high schools.

101
00:04:33,708 --> 00:04:35,187
'Including one
a victim went to.'

102
00:04:35,275 --> 00:04:37,842
- That's all I got.
- Alright.

103
00:04:37,929 --> 00:04:41,324
You mind waiting here?
I want to talk to him on my own.

104
00:04:41,411 --> 00:04:42,325
Sure.

105
00:05:04,347 --> 00:05:06,436
Richard Haibach?

106
00:05:06,523 --> 00:05:07,785
Who are you?

107
00:05:07,872 --> 00:05:09,352
I'm agent Lisbon
with the CBI.

108
00:05:09,439 --> 00:05:11,659
You mind if I ask you
a few questions?

109
00:05:11,746 --> 00:05:14,879
- Actually, I do.
- It shouldn't take long, sir.

110
00:05:14,966 --> 00:05:17,186
- Can I come in?
- Uh, not without a warrant.

111
00:05:17,273 --> 00:05:20,363
Okay. Uh, can you tell me
where you were last night?

112
00:05:20,450 --> 00:05:23,975
Home alone all night.
Why, was there another murder?

113
00:05:24,062 --> 00:05:26,630
I can't comment on
an ongoing investigation, sir.

114
00:05:26,717 --> 00:05:28,763
That means there was one.
I'm not dumb.

115
00:05:28,850 --> 00:05:30,591
I've said a hundred times
already.

116
00:05:30,678 --> 00:05:32,375
I've got nothing to do
with this.

117
00:05:32,462 --> 00:05:33,594
Sir, I didn't--

118
00:05:33,681 --> 00:05:35,160
You people just
won't let go.

119
00:05:35,247 --> 00:05:37,511
I'm getting tired being
harassed all the time.

120
00:05:37,598 --> 00:05:39,643
Mr. Haibach, I just
have a few questions.

121
00:05:39,730 --> 00:05:42,167
- Then I'll be on my way.
- I know the law.

122
00:05:42,254 --> 00:05:45,083
I don't have to answer your
questions or tell you anything.

123
00:06:00,055 --> 00:06:01,535
[cell phone keypad beeps]

124
00:06:01,622 --> 00:06:04,146
[rings]

125
00:06:04,233 --> 00:06:06,278
- Hey, boss.
- 'Anything? Updates?'

126
00:06:06,366 --> 00:06:09,064
Yeah, I checked out the other
two suspects on the list.

127
00:06:09,151 --> 00:06:11,109
'The first's been
out of state all week.'

128
00:06:11,196 --> 00:06:12,981
'The FAA flight list
confirms it.'

129
00:06:13,068 --> 00:06:15,287
'The second was with his wife,
kids, and in-laws.'

130
00:06:15,375 --> 00:06:17,855
- Anyone else on the list?
- 'Cho's working those.'

131
00:06:17,942 --> 00:06:20,336
Half alibied out straightaway.
He's starting on the other half.

132
00:06:20,423 --> 00:06:21,990
'Hold off. I want you'

133
00:06:22,077 --> 00:06:23,644
to canvas the crime scene.

134
00:06:23,731 --> 00:06:27,474
See if you can put Haibach
anywhere near there last night.

135
00:06:27,561 --> 00:06:29,650
So? Is he our guy?

136
00:06:29,737 --> 00:06:31,956
I didn't get
a good feeling from him.

137
00:06:32,043 --> 00:06:34,002
[keys jangle, ignition clicks]

138
00:06:36,134 --> 00:06:38,093
So we'll start with
an intro, you know.

139
00:06:38,180 --> 00:06:39,616
Background on
the San Joaquin killer

140
00:06:39,703 --> 00:06:41,879
and then I'll go into
questions for you two.

141
00:06:44,012 --> 00:06:46,580
Um, wait. Excuse me, gentlemen,
one moment, please.

142
00:06:46,667 --> 00:06:49,234
Paul, can you hold
the roll, please?

143
00:06:50,584 --> 00:06:54,196
Mr. Jane, how are you?

144
00:06:54,283 --> 00:06:56,198
- Hello, Karen.
- Okay, listen, I understand.

145
00:06:56,285 --> 00:06:59,288
But I have a new show now.
It's a whole new format.

146
00:06:59,375 --> 00:07:02,073
More intimate, none of that
"gotcha" journalism I did.

147
00:07:04,336 --> 00:07:06,991
Oh. What'd they do,
hold your shoes hostage?

148
00:07:07,078 --> 00:07:08,602
[chuckles]
Very funny.

149
00:07:08,689 --> 00:07:10,517
This week, I'm taking
an in-depth look

150
00:07:10,604 --> 00:07:13,563
at the San Joaquin killer.
I'm hoping you'll join me for

151
00:07:13,650 --> 00:07:16,044
the broadcast Thursday night,
offer your insight, experience.

152
00:07:16,131 --> 00:07:18,568
You don't need me. You have
special agent Wainright here.

153
00:07:18,655 --> 00:07:20,396
He's a good, honest man.

154
00:07:20,483 --> 00:07:23,181
Yes, but he doesn't pop
on camera the way you do.

155
00:07:23,268 --> 00:07:25,096
Oh, Karen, you flatter me.

156
00:07:25,183 --> 00:07:27,795
Good to see you. Be well.

157
00:07:27,882 --> 00:07:29,318
[sighs]

158
00:07:29,405 --> 00:07:31,146
Let's go.

159
00:07:31,233 --> 00:07:33,844
[clears throat]

160
00:07:33,931 --> 00:07:37,369
With me today is special agent
Luther Wainright of the CBI.

161
00:07:37,457 --> 00:07:39,241
Welcome,
and James Panzer

162
00:07:39,328 --> 00:07:41,330
an investigative crime
reporter and blogger.

163
00:07:41,417 --> 00:07:43,027
Mr. Panzer,
we'll start with you.

164
00:07:43,114 --> 00:07:46,117
You've been tracking the San
Joaquin killer for how long now?

165
00:07:46,204 --> 00:07:48,990
Well, it's been, uh, nearly
two years now, Karen.

166
00:07:49,077 --> 00:07:51,514
That's when he took
the life of Molly Maier

167
00:07:51,601 --> 00:07:54,952
a young girl who lived down the
street from me back in Fresno.

168
00:07:55,039 --> 00:07:57,520
Uh, that was his first victim
that we know of.

169
00:07:57,607 --> 00:08:00,088
And pursuing him is pretty much
your life now, right?

170
00:08:00,175 --> 00:08:02,699
You created a web site
devoted to his capture?

171
00:08:02,786 --> 00:08:05,441
Yes, that's right. Molly's
family, they're good people

172
00:08:05,528 --> 00:08:08,357
and they deserve justice. All
his victims deserve justice.

173
00:08:08,444 --> 00:08:11,142
I'm simply trying to help
achieve that goal.

174
00:08:11,229 --> 00:08:12,970
And s-special
Lieutenant Wainright..

175
00:08:13,057 --> 00:08:14,276
Special agent Wainright.

176
00:08:14,363 --> 00:08:16,147
Special Agent.
And special agent Wainright

177
00:08:16,234 --> 00:08:17,932
how close are you
to that goal?

178
00:08:18,019 --> 00:08:20,717
To arresting
the San Joaquin killer?

179
00:08:20,804 --> 00:08:23,372
Well, Karen, we are
absolutely making progress.

180
00:08:26,593 --> 00:08:27,942
[footsteps approaching]

181
00:08:30,074 --> 00:08:32,512
Working on a computer?

182
00:08:32,599 --> 00:08:34,644
What's next,
ray guns, teleporters?

183
00:08:34,731 --> 00:08:36,124
Mm.

184
00:08:36,211 --> 00:08:38,474
Hang on. Hey, Cho.

185
00:08:38,561 --> 00:08:40,041
'Haibach wasn't home
last night.'

186
00:08:40,128 --> 00:08:42,347
He was at a bodega near
the crime scene

187
00:08:42,434 --> 00:08:44,132
an hour before Michelle Karp
went missing.

188
00:08:44,219 --> 00:08:46,917
Owner I.D.'d him, said he was
parked in front

189
00:08:47,004 --> 00:08:48,745
for 20 minutes
before he went inside.

190
00:08:48,832 --> 00:08:50,530
How close to
the crime scene?

191
00:08:50,617 --> 00:08:52,836
Hey, how far
from the crime scene are we?

192
00:08:52,923 --> 00:08:54,316
[mouth full]
Six blocks.

193
00:08:54,403 --> 00:08:55,752
What?

194
00:08:55,839 --> 00:08:57,493
Six blocks.

195
00:08:57,580 --> 00:08:58,494
- Six blocks.
- 'Alright.'

196
00:08:58,581 --> 00:08:59,887
I'll work on Judge Waters.

197
00:08:59,974 --> 00:09:01,976
The proximity and the fact
that Haibach lied

198
00:09:02,063 --> 00:09:04,718
should be enough to get him
to sign a search warrant.

199
00:09:04,805 --> 00:09:05,719
'Okay.'

200
00:09:08,852 --> 00:09:10,288
[mouth full]
What?

201
00:09:10,375 --> 00:09:12,552
How do you not feel
that on your face?

202
00:09:12,639 --> 00:09:14,728
Feel what?

203
00:09:25,869 --> 00:09:27,392
[Patrick]
James Panzer?

204
00:09:27,479 --> 00:09:28,829
Patrick Jane.

205
00:09:28,916 --> 00:09:30,395
- Hello.
- Thanks for meeting with me.

206
00:09:30,482 --> 00:09:32,920
Oh, anything I can do to help
with the investigation.

207
00:09:33,007 --> 00:09:35,749
So, James,
what's your deal?

208
00:09:35,836 --> 00:09:38,621
You've devoted your life to
the capture of a serial killer.

209
00:09:38,708 --> 00:09:41,711
That's either very saintly
or very weird.

210
00:09:41,798 --> 00:09:43,713
Well, that's an odd notion
coming from you.

211
00:09:43,800 --> 00:09:45,846
And I know about you,
Mr. Jane.

212
00:09:45,933 --> 00:09:48,239
Ah, I thought you might.

213
00:09:48,326 --> 00:09:50,198
Red John did kill
my family.

214
00:09:50,285 --> 00:09:52,243
Uh, what is it
that gives you cause?

215
00:09:52,330 --> 00:09:54,202
What did Molly Maier
mean to you?

216
00:09:54,289 --> 00:09:56,508
I didn't know her.
I never spoke to her.

217
00:09:56,596 --> 00:09:59,033
I saw her at
the bus stop a few times.

218
00:09:59,120 --> 00:10:01,165
But, uh, it was
such a brutal act.

219
00:10:01,252 --> 00:10:04,255
It was so close to home that it
had a visceral impact

220
00:10:04,342 --> 00:10:06,823
on me that I couldn't shake.
I don't know why.

221
00:10:06,910 --> 00:10:08,912
I mean, people search
for reason or meaning

222
00:10:08,999 --> 00:10:11,393
behind events in their lives,
when more often than not

223
00:10:11,480 --> 00:10:13,264
there is none.

224
00:10:13,351 --> 00:10:16,093
Oh, uh, congratulations,
by the way.

225
00:10:16,180 --> 00:10:18,574
Thank you. For what?

226
00:10:18,661 --> 00:10:20,620
You killed Red John.

227
00:10:20,707 --> 00:10:23,623
Oh, that. Yes. Hmm.

228
00:10:23,710 --> 00:10:25,102
What was that like?

229
00:10:25,189 --> 00:10:28,497
Yeah, uh...not as satisfying
as you might think.

230
00:10:28,584 --> 00:10:31,892
Something occurred to me while
I was reading your web site.

231
00:10:31,979 --> 00:10:33,284
Yes?

232
00:10:33,371 --> 00:10:35,765
The people that post comments
on the site..

233
00:10:35,852 --> 00:10:38,550
Have you considered that one
of them might be the killer?

234
00:10:38,638 --> 00:10:42,424
Well, it's a possibility,
albeit a rather obvious one.

235
00:10:42,511 --> 00:10:44,469
You see, the San Joaquin
killer is not

236
00:10:44,556 --> 00:10:45,906
like other serial killers.

237
00:10:45,993 --> 00:10:48,560
He doesn't kill
for the same reasons.

238
00:10:48,648 --> 00:10:51,607
He commits murder solely
for the immediate pleasure

239
00:10:51,694 --> 00:10:53,478
that he takes in the act.

240
00:10:53,565 --> 00:10:55,959
The media,
other people's reactions..

241
00:10:56,046 --> 00:10:57,439
That's irrelevant to him.

242
00:10:57,526 --> 00:11:00,137
- A purist.
- Exactly.

243
00:11:00,224 --> 00:11:02,836
Consider the way
he murders his victims.

244
00:11:02,923 --> 00:11:06,753
He slits their throats
slowly, carefully

245
00:11:06,840 --> 00:11:09,930
so he can watch the life
drain out of their eyes.

246
00:11:11,496 --> 00:11:14,456
Uh, I apologize if I'm making
you uncomfortable.

247
00:11:14,543 --> 00:11:16,588
Oh, no.
It's in my wheelhouse.

248
00:11:16,676 --> 00:11:19,069
This killer has come
to consume me.

249
00:11:19,156 --> 00:11:20,114
[chuckles]

250
00:11:20,201 --> 00:11:22,638
Mr. Panzer,
how would you feel

251
00:11:22,725 --> 00:11:24,553
about helping me
with this investigation?

252
00:11:24,640 --> 00:11:27,034
[chuckles]
Well, there's nothing
I would like more.

253
00:11:27,121 --> 00:11:28,557
Excellent.

254
00:11:30,994 --> 00:11:35,042
Mr. Haibach, CBI. Open up.

255
00:11:35,129 --> 00:11:37,871
I told you, I'm through
answering your questions.

256
00:11:37,958 --> 00:11:39,176
I'm through being harassed.

257
00:11:39,263 --> 00:11:41,048
I have rights,
like anybody else, right?

258
00:11:41,135 --> 00:11:43,137
I don't wanna
have to talk to you!

259
00:11:43,224 --> 00:11:45,879
This is a warrant to search the
premises. Step aside, please.

260
00:11:52,450 --> 00:11:54,148
[lowered voice]
You clear the back.

261
00:11:54,235 --> 00:11:55,410
I'll wait here.

262
00:12:09,772 --> 00:12:11,556
[door creaks]

263
00:12:32,012 --> 00:12:33,100
[opens door]

264
00:12:35,493 --> 00:12:37,626
[door creaks]

265
00:12:41,195 --> 00:12:43,980
Abuse of power.
That's what this is.

266
00:12:51,727 --> 00:12:53,773
[click]

267
00:13:08,483 --> 00:13:11,399
[crackling]

268
00:13:15,142 --> 00:13:17,666
[inhales sharply]

269
00:13:21,757 --> 00:13:23,715
Step aside.

270
00:13:23,803 --> 00:13:26,196
[sniffles]

271
00:13:26,283 --> 00:13:28,198
I said, step aside!

272
00:13:33,551 --> 00:13:35,902
[sniffles and sobs]

273
00:13:38,426 --> 00:13:39,819
[clanking]

274
00:13:39,906 --> 00:13:42,169
Lisbon!

275
00:13:48,915 --> 00:13:50,655
[keyboard clicking]

276
00:13:50,742 --> 00:13:53,745
Did you I.D. any of
the women in the photos yet?

277
00:13:53,833 --> 00:13:56,052
Some, but we haven't
been able to match any

278
00:13:56,139 --> 00:13:57,662
to the San Joaquin
killer's victims.

279
00:13:57,749 --> 00:14:00,013
Haibach would have burned them
first. He's not stupid.

280
00:14:00,100 --> 00:14:03,016
Keep on looking.
His lawyer's on his way.

281
00:14:03,103 --> 00:14:04,844
[keyboard clicking]

282
00:14:09,413 --> 00:14:11,981
He won't talk
without his lawyer.

283
00:14:12,068 --> 00:14:15,028
He's been around the block
a few times with Fresno P.D.

284
00:14:15,115 --> 00:14:17,857
- 'Hmm.'
- "Hmm," what?

285
00:14:17,944 --> 00:14:20,337
He's a meticulous,
maladjusted loner

286
00:14:20,424 --> 00:14:23,863
who's into stalking young women,
and he has no alibi.

287
00:14:23,950 --> 00:14:25,952
'Yeah, he certainly
looks the part.'

288
00:14:26,039 --> 00:14:29,869
I like him for this.
I like him a lot.

289
00:14:31,087 --> 00:14:32,436
[door opens]

290
00:14:32,523 --> 00:14:33,829
[beep]

291
00:14:33,916 --> 00:14:36,484
[door closes]

292
00:14:36,571 --> 00:14:37,877
[Panzer]
'Hello?'

293
00:14:37,964 --> 00:14:39,922
Hi. It's Patrick Jane.

294
00:14:40,009 --> 00:14:42,142
What's your take
on Richard Haibach?

295
00:14:42,229 --> 00:14:43,970
Oh, he's, uh,
one of the suspects

296
00:14:44,057 --> 00:14:46,233
that the Fresno P.D.
identified.

297
00:14:46,320 --> 00:14:48,800
Antisocial, underachiever..

298
00:14:48,888 --> 00:14:51,455
He's viable, but he doesn't
have the intelligence I'd be

299
00:14:51,542 --> 00:14:52,804
'looking for in our man.'

300
00:14:52,892 --> 00:14:56,678
Thanks. I don't disagree.

301
00:14:56,765 --> 00:14:59,637
It's said that the truest motive
for a serial killer

302
00:14:59,724 --> 00:15:01,335
often lies with
the first victim.

303
00:15:01,422 --> 00:15:02,553
Molly Maier.

304
00:15:02,640 --> 00:15:04,294
I was wondering
if you could

305
00:15:04,381 --> 00:15:05,643
introduce me to her family.

306
00:15:05,730 --> 00:15:08,255
I'd be happy to.
Give me an hour or so.

307
00:15:08,342 --> 00:15:10,953
Thanks. Then maybe we can go
over your research?

308
00:15:11,040 --> 00:15:12,563
'Yeah, sure.'

309
00:15:12,650 --> 00:15:15,523
You could come by my apartment
after we visit the Maiers.

310
00:15:15,610 --> 00:15:18,047
- 'Thanks.'
- Okay.

311
00:15:20,049 --> 00:15:21,921
[snaps shut]

312
00:15:34,455 --> 00:15:36,805
Molly had just turned 16
when she was killed.

313
00:15:36,892 --> 00:15:38,807
She didn't even have
her driver's license.

314
00:15:38,894 --> 00:15:42,028
She was walking home
from her girlfriend's house.

315
00:15:42,115 --> 00:15:44,552
Never made it home.

316
00:15:44,639 --> 00:15:48,686
We've been following
the investigation.

317
00:15:48,773 --> 00:15:51,559
Jim's been good enough to keep
us up-to-date with the others.

318
00:15:51,646 --> 00:15:54,170
[voice breaking]
All those girls.

319
00:15:54,257 --> 00:15:57,130
I can't believe nobody's been
able to catch this monster.

320
00:15:57,217 --> 00:15:59,480
We will, soon.

321
00:16:00,960 --> 00:16:02,700
I don't know.

322
00:16:04,528 --> 00:16:07,096
I don't know.

323
00:16:07,183 --> 00:16:09,969
Now don't lose hope. Molly
wouldn't want that, would she?

324
00:16:11,971 --> 00:16:14,408
No, she wouldn't.

325
00:16:16,671 --> 00:16:21,676
This killer will be brought
to justice. You have my word.

326
00:16:21,763 --> 00:16:24,461
You think I could take a look
at Molly's bedroom?

327
00:16:24,548 --> 00:16:26,333
No. I don't want...

328
00:16:29,901 --> 00:16:32,078
of course.

329
00:16:32,165 --> 00:16:34,994
Tom, will you
show him upstairs?

330
00:16:37,953 --> 00:16:40,129
Thank you.

331
00:16:40,216 --> 00:16:42,218
[whispers]
It's okay.

332
00:16:50,748 --> 00:16:52,359
Sorry about the mess.

333
00:16:52,446 --> 00:16:55,449
We really should get around
to cleaning it up.

334
00:16:55,536 --> 00:16:58,713
Truth is, neither of us
has the heart for it.

335
00:17:09,506 --> 00:17:12,901
"Crap recital practice."

336
00:17:19,951 --> 00:17:22,215
D-do you mind, uh...

337
00:17:22,302 --> 00:17:24,130
Oh, uh, sure.

338
00:17:24,217 --> 00:17:25,957
Thank you.

339
00:17:26,045 --> 00:17:28,221
She loved to dance.

340
00:17:28,308 --> 00:17:32,442
That was, uh...
her thing.

341
00:17:32,529 --> 00:17:34,923
[click]

342
00:17:40,276 --> 00:17:43,584
Worked really hard at it.

343
00:17:43,671 --> 00:17:46,456
[Louis Armstrong's "What
A Wonderful World" playing]

344
00:17:49,590 --> 00:17:53,376
♪ I see trees of green ♪

345
00:17:53,463 --> 00:17:57,032
♪ Red roses, too ♪

346
00:17:57,119 --> 00:17:59,687
♪ I see them bloom ♪

347
00:17:59,774 --> 00:18:02,733
♪ For me and you ♪

348
00:18:02,820 --> 00:18:07,390
♪ And I think to myself ♪

349
00:18:07,477 --> 00:18:10,001
♪ What a wonderful world ♪

350
00:18:10,089 --> 00:18:12,134
Once she decided
she wanted to do something

351
00:18:12,221 --> 00:18:14,615
there was no stopping her.

352
00:18:14,702 --> 00:18:18,662
Single-minded,
determined.

353
00:18:18,749 --> 00:18:21,535
Just like her mother.

354
00:18:21,622 --> 00:18:23,798
♪ And clouds of white ♪

355
00:18:23,885 --> 00:18:25,539
♪ The bright blessed day ♪

356
00:18:25,626 --> 00:18:29,804
Must have heard this song
a hundred times.

357
00:18:29,891 --> 00:18:34,330
♪ And I think to myself ♪

358
00:18:34,417 --> 00:18:37,986
♪ What a wonderful world ♪

359
00:18:42,947 --> 00:18:46,081
♪ The colors of the rainbow ♪

360
00:18:46,168 --> 00:18:49,302
♪ So pretty in the sky ♪

361
00:18:49,389 --> 00:18:53,044
♪ Are also on the faces ♪

362
00:18:53,132 --> 00:18:56,047
♪ Of people going by ♪♪

363
00:18:57,179 --> 00:18:58,572
[Lisbon]
'Counselor?'

364
00:18:58,659 --> 00:19:00,835
We found dozens of pictures
of young women

365
00:19:00,922 --> 00:19:02,445
in your client's house.

366
00:19:02,532 --> 00:19:04,447
That's your justification
for holding him for murder?

367
00:19:04,534 --> 00:19:06,536
That he took pictures?
He's a photographer.

368
00:19:06,623 --> 00:19:10,149
Sexually suggestive photographs
taken without consent.

369
00:19:10,236 --> 00:19:12,760
Taken in public
without any complaints.

370
00:19:12,847 --> 00:19:14,979
That's not even a misdemeanor.
That's a hobby.

371
00:19:15,066 --> 00:19:17,504
He lied about his whereabouts
the night of the murder.

372
00:19:17,591 --> 00:19:19,201
- I was out taking photos.
- Shh.

373
00:19:19,288 --> 00:19:20,942
[clears throat]

374
00:19:21,029 --> 00:19:23,553
Not exactly a capital offense.

375
00:19:23,640 --> 00:19:26,556
Meanwhile, my client has
had to endure

376
00:19:26,643 --> 00:19:28,950
continued police harassment
for a crime he didn't commit.

377
00:19:29,037 --> 00:19:30,604
I did not kill those girls!

378
00:19:30,691 --> 00:19:32,997
Richard, do not
say another word.

379
00:19:33,084 --> 00:19:36,436
Agent Lisbon,
either release him now

380
00:19:36,523 --> 00:19:39,874
or I'm bringing a harassment
lawsuit against you and the CBI

381
00:19:39,961 --> 00:19:42,311
first thing tomorrow morning.

382
00:19:44,661 --> 00:19:47,925
Mr. Haibach,
you're free to go.

383
00:19:48,012 --> 00:19:49,231
[thumps table]

384
00:19:49,318 --> 00:19:50,363
Thank you.

385
00:19:52,365 --> 00:19:54,454
[sighs]

386
00:19:57,108 --> 00:19:58,588
[sighs]

387
00:20:00,808 --> 00:20:03,680
[footsteps recede]

388
00:20:05,508 --> 00:20:08,859
My apartment's upstairs.
Pardon the mess.

389
00:20:08,946 --> 00:20:11,601
[door opens]

390
00:20:11,688 --> 00:20:13,342
[door creaks]

391
00:20:15,518 --> 00:20:16,998
Wow.

392
00:20:18,086 --> 00:20:20,044
[door closes]

393
00:20:20,131 --> 00:20:23,004
Newspaper articles,
background on the victims

394
00:20:23,091 --> 00:20:26,268
interviews with
the victims' families...enjoy.

395
00:20:26,355 --> 00:20:28,314
Hard to know
where to start.

396
00:20:28,401 --> 00:20:30,011
Uh, water? Soda? Coffee?

397
00:20:30,098 --> 00:20:32,274
Water would be great.
Can I just dig in?

398
00:20:32,361 --> 00:20:33,319
Oh, be my guest.

399
00:20:33,406 --> 00:20:35,799
Thank you.

400
00:20:35,886 --> 00:20:38,802
[exhales deeply]

401
00:20:42,589 --> 00:20:44,939
[mutters under breath]
Crime scene...

402
00:21:00,824 --> 00:21:02,435
[clatters]

403
00:21:02,522 --> 00:21:05,351
[Louis Armstrong's "What A Wonderful World" playing]

404
00:21:07,875 --> 00:21:12,488
♪ I see trees of green ♪

405
00:21:12,575 --> 00:21:15,709
Hope you don't mind.
Music helps me think.

406
00:21:15,796 --> 00:21:19,365
Oh, not at all.
I'll put the water over here.

407
00:21:19,452 --> 00:21:22,324
♪ For me and you ♪

408
00:21:22,411 --> 00:21:25,109
♪ And I think to myself ♪

409
00:21:27,808 --> 00:21:31,551
♪ What a wonderful world ♪

410
00:21:33,640 --> 00:21:36,686
The more I think about it,
the more I'm starting to believe

411
00:21:36,773 --> 00:21:38,775
that your analysis of
the San Joaquin killer

412
00:21:38,862 --> 00:21:40,211
may be wrong.

413
00:21:40,299 --> 00:21:42,039
How so?

414
00:21:42,126 --> 00:21:43,650
Well, you said he's a purist.

415
00:21:43,737 --> 00:21:46,653
A man who kills simply
for the pleasure of it.

416
00:21:46,740 --> 00:21:51,266
I'm starting to think
he's a deeply damaged man

417
00:21:51,353 --> 00:21:53,355
who kills out of
a need for attention.

418
00:21:53,442 --> 00:21:56,402
♪ What a wonderful world ♪

419
00:21:56,489 --> 00:21:59,187
Neglected as a child,
I'd guess.

420
00:21:59,274 --> 00:22:02,146
What do you think, Jim?

421
00:22:02,233 --> 00:22:04,758
Well, I think
you couldn't be more wrong.

422
00:22:04,845 --> 00:22:07,108
♪ So pretty in the sky ♪

423
00:22:07,195 --> 00:22:08,979
look, I know
the San Joaquin killer

424
00:22:09,066 --> 00:22:11,155
better than anyone,
as you yourself have said.

425
00:22:11,242 --> 00:22:15,377
I've spent over a year
inhabiting his mind.

426
00:22:15,464 --> 00:22:17,510
This man is brilliant.

427
00:22:17,597 --> 00:22:20,643
He's a genius who's run circles
around the police.

428
00:22:20,730 --> 00:22:23,342
He's killed at will
without repercussion.

429
00:22:23,429 --> 00:22:26,388
♪ They're really saying ♪

430
00:22:26,475 --> 00:22:30,610
This is a man to be feared,
not pitied.

431
00:22:30,697 --> 00:22:32,133
[cell phone rings]

432
00:22:32,220 --> 00:22:34,135
Excuse me.

433
00:22:34,222 --> 00:22:36,920
[ringing continues]

434
00:22:37,007 --> 00:22:38,618
Hello?

435
00:22:38,705 --> 00:22:43,144
[Lisbon]
Jane?
S.F.P.D. found another body.

436
00:22:43,231 --> 00:22:44,885
♪ And I think to myself ♪

437
00:22:44,972 --> 00:22:47,061
Okay.

438
00:22:47,148 --> 00:22:49,585
♪ What a wonderful world ♪

439
00:22:49,672 --> 00:22:52,893
It's him, isn't it?

440
00:22:52,980 --> 00:22:55,548
He's killed again.

441
00:23:00,814 --> 00:23:04,731
Name's Jill Reisert, 18.
It's the San Joaquin killer.

442
00:23:04,818 --> 00:23:07,037
Hands, feet bound
with the same wire

443
00:23:07,124 --> 00:23:08,387
tied in the same knot.

444
00:23:08,474 --> 00:23:10,867
Killed elsewhere,
dumped randomly.

445
00:23:10,954 --> 00:23:12,042
Pebbles on the eyes?

446
00:23:12,129 --> 00:23:14,741
No. No eyes.

447
00:23:20,616 --> 00:23:22,270
[exhales deeply]

448
00:23:24,185 --> 00:23:27,231
Only days
after the last murder.

449
00:23:27,318 --> 00:23:29,886
He has a taste
for attention now.

450
00:23:29,973 --> 00:23:32,672
Bigger city, bigger stage,
bigger violence.

451
00:23:32,759 --> 00:23:34,543
He's getting more
and more excited.

452
00:23:34,630 --> 00:23:36,197
What time was she killed?

453
00:23:36,284 --> 00:23:38,765
Based on the rigor, coroner
thinks it was this morning.

454
00:23:38,852 --> 00:23:41,071
Haibach was free by then.
He could've done it.

455
00:23:41,158 --> 00:23:43,726
No. I know who did this.

456
00:23:43,813 --> 00:23:45,162
James Panzer.

457
00:23:45,249 --> 00:23:48,122
Panzer? The reporter
covering the case?

458
00:23:48,209 --> 00:23:50,429
The Fresno P.D.
would've checked him out.

459
00:23:50,516 --> 00:23:53,214
They did. He had an alibi
for two of the murders.

460
00:23:53,301 --> 00:23:55,999
Both faked, somehow. Look into
them if you don't believe me.

461
00:23:56,086 --> 00:23:58,567
Or don't. I don't care.
I know it was him.

462
00:23:58,654 --> 00:24:00,743
- Evidence.
- That's the problem.

463
00:24:00,830 --> 00:24:03,267
We don't have anything
linking him to the crime.

464
00:24:03,354 --> 00:24:05,095
FBI's pushing to
take over this case.

465
00:24:05,182 --> 00:24:07,402
I'm doing everything
I can to hold them off

466
00:24:07,489 --> 00:24:09,926
but I don't have the pull.
We need an arrest.

467
00:24:10,013 --> 00:24:11,537
I'll get him.
I need time.

468
00:24:11,624 --> 00:24:13,756
- Time we don't have.
- I'll get him.

469
00:24:13,843 --> 00:24:16,193
Okay. How long do you need?

470
00:24:16,280 --> 00:24:18,587
I'll have him in custody
by the morning.

471
00:24:21,068 --> 00:24:24,158
Give you till the morning.
Good luck.

472
00:24:26,682 --> 00:24:29,468
Panzer knows everything
about this case.

473
00:24:29,555 --> 00:24:32,253
'We've got no forensic evidence
and no eyewitnesses.'

474
00:24:32,340 --> 00:24:34,211
How do you propose
we catch him?

475
00:24:34,298 --> 00:24:36,170
We take away
what he wants most.

476
00:24:47,094 --> 00:24:50,010
Mr. Jane.

477
00:24:51,098 --> 00:24:52,882
Any news?

478
00:24:52,969 --> 00:24:54,710
Uh, n-not now, Karen.

479
00:24:54,797 --> 00:24:57,104
Please, give us
a morsel, anything.

480
00:25:04,503 --> 00:25:07,244
Okay. We have a suspect.

481
00:25:07,331 --> 00:25:09,029
We'll be making an arrest
sometime tonight.

482
00:25:09,116 --> 00:25:11,074
We'll announce it to the press
tomorrow morning.

483
00:25:11,161 --> 00:25:13,816
- Who is it?
- You'll find out tomorrow.

484
00:25:13,903 --> 00:25:16,906
Come on. Just tell me if it's
one of the previous suspects.

485
00:25:16,993 --> 00:25:18,517
[scoffs]

486
00:25:18,604 --> 00:25:20,431
It is, isn't it?

487
00:25:22,564 --> 00:25:25,480
- You didn't hear it from me.
- You can trust me.

488
00:25:25,567 --> 00:25:28,178
I mean it. You can't
go public with this today.

489
00:25:28,265 --> 00:25:30,224
I'm only telling you
so you're not caught

490
00:25:30,311 --> 00:25:31,399
off guard
tomorrow morning.

491
00:25:31,486 --> 00:25:35,490
Thank you. Wait. Patrick.

492
00:25:35,577 --> 00:25:38,362
The offer's still open
if you wanna come on my show.

493
00:25:38,449 --> 00:25:40,756
Uh, yeah, thank you,
but no, thanks.

494
00:25:40,843 --> 00:25:42,236
Think about it.
That's all.

495
00:25:42,323 --> 00:25:43,672
Sure.

496
00:25:43,759 --> 00:25:47,371
Dan. Dan.

497
00:25:47,458 --> 00:25:50,113
Call the office
and clear the 7:00 open.

498
00:25:50,200 --> 00:25:52,028
I've got a breaking story.

499
00:25:52,115 --> 00:25:54,378
Alright.
What's going on?

500
00:25:54,465 --> 00:25:56,467
Panzer committed these
murders out of hubris.

501
00:25:56,555 --> 00:25:59,253
He wants to create a mythology
around the San Joaquin killer.

502
00:25:59,340 --> 00:26:01,560
That's why he started
the web site.

503
00:26:01,647 --> 00:26:03,039
He doesn't just wanna kill.

504
00:26:03,126 --> 00:26:05,085
- He wants people to be in awe.
- Yes?

505
00:26:05,172 --> 00:26:07,130
So we arrest someone else
for the murders.

506
00:26:07,217 --> 00:26:08,784
Someone pathetic and unworthy.

507
00:26:08,871 --> 00:26:11,526
Thus destroying his mythology
with one fatal blow.

508
00:26:11,613 --> 00:26:14,616
Panzer will go out and kill
again just to prove us wrong.

509
00:26:14,703 --> 00:26:16,531
And we'll be there
waiting for him.

510
00:26:16,618 --> 00:26:19,229
Arrest somebody else?

511
00:26:19,316 --> 00:26:20,666
Oh..

512
00:26:22,015 --> 00:26:23,146
Are you sure, boss?

513
00:26:23,233 --> 00:26:25,496
I got this.

514
00:26:25,584 --> 00:26:27,324
Oh, come on.

515
00:26:27,411 --> 00:26:28,935
You people just don't quit..
Oh!

516
00:26:29,022 --> 00:26:30,240
You're under arrest.

517
00:26:30,327 --> 00:26:31,720
For what?

518
00:26:31,807 --> 00:26:32,808
We'll think of something.

519
00:26:32,895 --> 00:26:34,375
[Haibach groans]

520
00:26:34,462 --> 00:26:35,419
[doorbell chimes]

521
00:26:35,506 --> 00:26:36,943
[Panzer]
Hello?

522
00:26:37,030 --> 00:26:39,728
Mr. Panzer, it's Patrick.
I have some information.

523
00:26:39,815 --> 00:26:42,557
[buzzer buzzing]

524
00:26:44,472 --> 00:26:46,605
we got him.

525
00:26:46,692 --> 00:26:48,998
San Joaquin killer.
Made an arrest.

526
00:26:49,085 --> 00:26:52,262
I heard about it on the news,
but I didn't believe it.

527
00:26:52,349 --> 00:26:53,829
- Who is it?
- Richard Haibach.

528
00:26:53,916 --> 00:26:56,049
- I thought he had an alibi.
- Faked.

529
00:26:56,136 --> 00:26:58,355
Lied about the whole thing,
which isn't difficult to

530
00:26:58,442 --> 00:27:00,401
pull off if you know
what you're doing.

531
00:27:00,488 --> 00:27:01,881
You're certain
that it's him?

532
00:27:01,968 --> 00:27:03,970
We found pictures of
women in his home.

533
00:27:04,057 --> 00:27:05,972
Two of the victims
were amongst them.

534
00:27:06,059 --> 00:27:07,669
That doesn't prove
he's the killer.

535
00:27:07,756 --> 00:27:10,890
We found something else.
I can't comment on specifics.

536
00:27:10,977 --> 00:27:13,806
But the A.G.'s office
is confident that it

537
00:27:13,893 --> 00:27:16,417
along with Haibach's proximity
to the Karp murder

538
00:27:16,504 --> 00:27:19,899
and the photos will be enough
for a conviction.

539
00:27:19,986 --> 00:27:22,292
We'll announce it to the press
tomorrow morning.

540
00:27:22,379 --> 00:27:24,164
I wanted you to know first,

541
00:27:24,251 --> 00:27:26,732
given your...
personal attachment to the case.

542
00:27:28,429 --> 00:27:31,475
And I wanted to stop by
and say thanks for your help.

543
00:27:31,562 --> 00:27:33,913
Oh, yes, of course.

544
00:27:34,000 --> 00:27:36,524
Look, can I get you
a drink or something?

545
00:27:36,611 --> 00:27:39,832
No, thanks. Do you mind
if I use your restroom?

546
00:27:39,919 --> 00:27:41,616
Oh, sure, sure.
Right-right over there.

547
00:27:41,703 --> 00:27:44,445
- End of the hall here?
- Yes.

548
00:27:52,671 --> 00:27:54,585
[toilet flushes]

549
00:27:57,023 --> 00:27:59,634
[cabinet door creaks]

550
00:28:18,522 --> 00:28:20,524
[switch clicks]

551
00:28:23,266 --> 00:28:28,228
You sure you don't want
a drink or a snack?

552
00:28:28,315 --> 00:28:31,753
Oh, no. I have to get going.

553
00:28:31,840 --> 00:28:32,798
[sighs]

554
00:28:32,885 --> 00:28:34,190
[door opens]

555
00:28:34,277 --> 00:28:36,410
You know,
it's funny, isn't it?

556
00:28:36,497 --> 00:28:38,064
What's that?

557
00:28:38,151 --> 00:28:40,980
We were both wrong
about the San Joaquin killer.

558
00:28:41,067 --> 00:28:44,984
He wasn't a purist, nor was he
a man desperate for attention.

559
00:28:45,071 --> 00:28:47,029
He was just a sick,
sexually deranged man

560
00:28:47,116 --> 00:28:49,640
that likes to take pictures
of little girls.

561
00:28:49,728 --> 00:28:53,209
Simple-minded pervert.
Goodnight.

562
00:28:55,211 --> 00:28:57,605
[door closes]

563
00:29:18,539 --> 00:29:20,410
[whispers]
Come on.

564
00:29:20,497 --> 00:29:22,021
[door opens]

565
00:29:22,108 --> 00:29:24,414
[door closes]

566
00:29:25,720 --> 00:29:28,027
There he is.

567
00:29:34,207 --> 00:29:35,512
[alarm chirps]

568
00:29:41,780 --> 00:29:44,260
[engine starts]

569
00:29:45,566 --> 00:29:47,220
Wait.

570
00:29:53,269 --> 00:29:56,142
[alert chiming,
seat belts click]

571
00:30:15,596 --> 00:30:18,991
[Lisbon]
I'll call S.F.P.D. for backup.

572
00:30:25,127 --> 00:30:26,041
[whispering]
Jane!

573
00:30:59,553 --> 00:31:03,165
[whispering]
Um...Lisbon?

574
00:31:28,625 --> 00:31:30,062
Couldn't stay away,
could you?

575
00:31:30,149 --> 00:31:31,585
[desk clangs]

576
00:31:31,672 --> 00:31:34,501
Put your hands in the air.
Step back from the table.

577
00:31:34,588 --> 00:31:35,894
What are you doing here?

578
00:31:35,981 --> 00:31:38,374
I said put your hands
in the air now!

579
00:31:38,461 --> 00:31:40,333
Look, you don't understand.
I j--

580
00:31:43,249 --> 00:31:46,252
What are you doing here?

581
00:31:46,339 --> 00:31:47,906
Turn the camera off.

582
00:31:47,993 --> 00:31:50,299
Karen called me
about an hour ago.

583
00:31:50,386 --> 00:31:53,346
She gave me this address.
She asked me to meet her here.

584
00:31:53,433 --> 00:31:55,914
I've never been
here before in my life.

585
00:31:58,003 --> 00:32:01,267
- I said turn the camera off.
- Turn it off.

586
00:32:03,747 --> 00:32:06,750
- You called him?
- He's the expert.

587
00:32:06,837 --> 00:32:08,883
I wanted him to authenticate
that this was

588
00:32:08,970 --> 00:32:10,798
'the kill room used
by the killer.'

589
00:32:10,885 --> 00:32:12,582
How did you know
about this place?

590
00:32:12,669 --> 00:32:16,064
She was contacted by him,
by the San Joaquin killer.

591
00:32:16,151 --> 00:32:18,632
Yes. Just after
my 7:00 broadcast

592
00:32:18,719 --> 00:32:21,330
'during which I announced
the CBI had someone in custody.'

593
00:32:21,417 --> 00:32:23,245
Someone texted me
from a blocked number

594
00:32:23,332 --> 00:32:26,292
said he was the S.J.K.
and gave me this address.

595
00:32:26,379 --> 00:32:27,684
Alright. Out.

596
00:32:27,771 --> 00:32:30,992
This is officially
a CBI crime scene. Let's go!

597
00:32:37,477 --> 00:32:39,131
[breathes deeply]

598
00:32:58,237 --> 00:33:00,891
I tried to tell you
you had the wrong man.

599
00:33:00,979 --> 00:33:02,981
You set this up.

600
00:33:03,068 --> 00:33:05,070
You knew we were
following you.

601
00:33:05,157 --> 00:33:06,897
I don't know what
you're talking about

602
00:33:06,985 --> 00:33:09,291
but I admit,
I'm very hurt by all this.

603
00:33:09,378 --> 00:33:11,815
I mean, here I thought
you were seeking my help.

604
00:33:11,902 --> 00:33:13,295
That we were kindred spirits.

605
00:33:13,382 --> 00:33:15,950
Where all along
you were just lying to me.

606
00:33:16,037 --> 00:33:17,473
You were using me.

607
00:33:17,560 --> 00:33:20,999
That kind of duplicity..

608
00:33:21,086 --> 00:33:24,045
Leading a double life,
I don't know how you do that.

609
00:33:44,761 --> 00:33:46,676
'Agent Lisbon, Jane.'

610
00:33:46,763 --> 00:33:50,289
I'd like you to meet
FBI special agent Susan Darcy.

611
00:33:50,376 --> 00:33:52,595
As of this morning,
her team will be

612
00:33:52,682 --> 00:33:53,944
taking over the investigation.

613
00:33:54,032 --> 00:33:56,077
- Sir--
- No, it's okay. It's fine.

614
00:33:56,164 --> 00:33:58,079
The man you want
is James Panzer.

615
00:34:00,342 --> 00:34:02,518
What sort of evidence
do you have on Panzer?

616
00:34:02,605 --> 00:34:04,738
- Well, nothing--
- Nothing concrete.

617
00:34:04,825 --> 00:34:07,654
Not, uh, your kind
of evidence, anyhow.

618
00:34:07,741 --> 00:34:09,917
How about your kind
of evidence?

619
00:34:11,788 --> 00:34:14,008
His medicine cabinet
is too neat.

620
00:34:17,229 --> 00:34:19,492
That's an interesting
perspective.

621
00:34:19,579 --> 00:34:21,015
I'll absorb the case files

622
00:34:21,102 --> 00:34:23,104
and if Panzer looks like
a good unsub

623
00:34:23,191 --> 00:34:26,455
you can be rest assured
that we will be on him.

624
00:34:26,542 --> 00:34:29,589
- No, you won't.
- Jane?

625
00:34:29,676 --> 00:34:32,679
While she's absorbing the case
files, Panzer will kill again.

626
00:34:32,766 --> 00:34:34,289
You're not being
constructive right now.

627
00:34:34,376 --> 00:34:36,987
- I'm just being honest.
- It's okay.

628
00:34:37,075 --> 00:34:38,467
I understand you're upset.

629
00:34:38,554 --> 00:34:40,295
You have every reason
to be upset.

630
00:34:40,382 --> 00:34:43,429
I would be, too, if someone
was taking over my case.

631
00:34:43,516 --> 00:34:45,909
If he does kill again,
it will be my responsibility,

632
00:34:45,996 --> 00:34:47,955
And I will answer for it.

633
00:34:48,042 --> 00:34:49,739
But let me be very clear.

634
00:34:49,826 --> 00:34:53,874
This investigation is now under
the jurisdiction of the FBI.

635
00:34:53,961 --> 00:34:55,223
It's all yours.

636
00:35:01,099 --> 00:35:03,405
I need those case files
as soon as possible.

637
00:35:03,492 --> 00:35:05,451
Yeah.

638
00:35:07,888 --> 00:35:08,845
Jane.

639
00:35:09,933 --> 00:35:11,457
Jane, you heard Wainright.

640
00:35:11,544 --> 00:35:13,676
Our hands are tied.

641
00:35:13,763 --> 00:35:15,113
Yeah.

642
00:35:18,290 --> 00:35:20,030
Yeah. Maybe yours are.

643
00:35:25,340 --> 00:35:27,212
[jazz music]

644
00:35:29,518 --> 00:35:32,782
Well, Karen, no disrespect
to the CBI or the FBI

645
00:35:32,869 --> 00:35:35,568
but it's not a good sign
when a case as complex

646
00:35:35,655 --> 00:35:36,960
and important as this one

647
00:35:37,047 --> 00:35:39,572
is shuttled
from agency to agency.

648
00:35:39,659 --> 00:35:42,836
There's, there's a lack of
communication and consistency.

649
00:35:42,923 --> 00:35:45,926
There's a pointless
replication of effort.

650
00:35:46,013 --> 00:35:49,234
You see the same problems in the
search for the zodiac killer.

651
00:35:49,321 --> 00:35:50,670
Uh, hold that thought, James.

652
00:35:50,757 --> 00:35:53,063
We'll be right back
after a quick break

653
00:35:53,151 --> 00:35:56,763
with our guest, James Panzer,
and a surprise special guest

654
00:35:56,850 --> 00:35:59,722
CBI consultant
Patrick Jane.

655
00:35:59,809 --> 00:36:01,071
And clear.

656
00:36:01,159 --> 00:36:02,812
I hope you don't mind.

657
00:36:02,899 --> 00:36:04,292
But--

658
00:36:04,379 --> 00:36:05,511
Patrick.

659
00:36:05,598 --> 00:36:08,427
Thank you so much
for coming, really.

660
00:36:09,863 --> 00:36:12,170
Hi.

661
00:36:12,257 --> 00:36:13,693
We're getting Mr. Jane
mic-ed?

662
00:36:13,780 --> 00:36:14,911
Yes, we are.

663
00:36:17,914 --> 00:36:21,614
[male #1]
In five, four, three, two...

664
00:36:21,701 --> 00:36:23,355
[jazz music]

665
00:36:23,442 --> 00:36:27,359
Joining us now on this S.J.K.
special edition is Patrick Jane

666
00:36:27,446 --> 00:36:29,491
'a consultant with the CBI'

667
00:36:29,578 --> 00:36:32,929
who has his own amazing story
of terrible tragedy

668
00:36:33,016 --> 00:36:35,454
and cold revenge. Patrick,
thank you for joining us.

669
00:36:35,541 --> 00:36:37,412
Oh, it's my pleasure, Karen.
James.

670
00:36:37,499 --> 00:36:39,849
In a nutshell,
why has the San Joaquin killer

671
00:36:39,936 --> 00:36:41,851
proven so difficult
to apprehend?

672
00:36:41,938 --> 00:36:43,331
Well, James is exactly right.

673
00:36:43,418 --> 00:36:45,377
There are too many cooks
in the kitchen.

674
00:36:45,464 --> 00:36:47,335
But I-I would also like to add

675
00:36:47,422 --> 00:36:49,642
that the San Joaquin killer
is very likely someone

676
00:36:49,729 --> 00:36:52,340
that has attached themselves
to the investigation somehow.

677
00:36:52,427 --> 00:36:55,561
Someone, uh, hiding
in plain sight.

678
00:36:55,648 --> 00:36:57,476
That's interesting.
Is there anyone

679
00:36:57,563 --> 00:36:59,042
in particular
that you're thinking of?

680
00:36:59,129 --> 00:37:00,087
We have our suspicions.

681
00:37:00,174 --> 00:37:02,089
Well, that sounds promising.

682
00:37:02,176 --> 00:37:04,439
Can we expect an arrest
anytime soon?

683
00:37:04,526 --> 00:37:05,745
Very likely.

684
00:37:05,832 --> 00:37:08,443
Karen, if I may ask Jane
a question, I'd--

685
00:37:08,530 --> 00:37:10,228
- Could you hold that thought?
- Well, I--

686
00:37:10,315 --> 00:37:12,186
Can-can you elaborate
on that, Patrick?

687
00:37:12,273 --> 00:37:14,449
What makes you think that
the San Joaquin killer

688
00:37:14,536 --> 00:37:15,929
will be caught soon?

689
00:37:16,016 --> 00:37:18,540
Well, he's become wrapped up
in his own mythology.

690
00:37:18,627 --> 00:37:21,848
Uh, he's drank his own
Kool-Aid, so to speak.

691
00:37:21,935 --> 00:37:25,286
Well, there is absolutely
nothing to suggest that.

692
00:37:25,373 --> 00:37:27,245
You're right, James,
I have no evidence.

693
00:37:27,332 --> 00:37:29,247
Just a good sense
of the man.

694
00:37:29,334 --> 00:37:32,032
The San Joaquin killer
is a sad little person

695
00:37:32,119 --> 00:37:34,991
living in a fantasy world
of power and prestige.

696
00:37:35,078 --> 00:37:38,517
It's only a matter of time
before he makes a mistake.

697
00:37:38,604 --> 00:37:40,736
Jim?

698
00:37:40,823 --> 00:37:43,478
I'd like to say I believe
what Mr. Jane is saying

699
00:37:43,565 --> 00:37:45,611
but I can't disagree more.

700
00:37:45,698 --> 00:37:49,441
I mean, this man is smart,
he is rational

701
00:37:49,528 --> 00:37:50,833
he's brilliant, in fact.

702
00:37:50,920 --> 00:37:53,967
Hmm. I-I respect your skills
as a journalist

703
00:37:54,054 --> 00:37:57,057
and as a blogger, James,
but I do this for a living.

704
00:37:57,144 --> 00:38:00,016
I don't think you know
or understand this man at all.

705
00:38:00,103 --> 00:38:02,715
Well, that's absurd!
I mean, that's just...

706
00:38:02,802 --> 00:38:07,459
That's... your opinion,
and I respect that.

707
00:38:07,546 --> 00:38:08,808
But, Patrick, I mean,

708
00:38:08,895 --> 00:38:10,853
I think you should
consider the plain facts.

709
00:38:10,940 --> 00:38:12,551
James, hold that thought.

710
00:38:12,638 --> 00:38:14,292
If I could just
respond here.

711
00:38:14,379 --> 00:38:16,816
You'll have that chance
when we come back with more

712
00:38:16,903 --> 00:38:19,645
on the search for
the San Joaquin killer.

713
00:38:19,732 --> 00:38:21,864
[male #1]
'We're clear.
30 seconds, everyone.'

714
00:38:21,951 --> 00:38:23,475
Great stuff, guys.

715
00:38:37,924 --> 00:38:41,667
I know what you're doing.

716
00:38:41,754 --> 00:38:43,059
You think I'm stupid?

717
00:38:43,146 --> 00:38:45,584
Yes.

718
00:38:45,671 --> 00:38:48,021
You're not going to
ruin this for me.

719
00:38:50,458 --> 00:38:52,852
When we come back,
Jim, you're up.

720
00:38:52,939 --> 00:38:54,636
[male #1]
'Ten seconds.'

721
00:38:56,116 --> 00:38:58,814
'In five, four'

722
00:38:58,901 --> 00:39:01,077
'three, two..'

723
00:39:01,164 --> 00:39:03,210
James Panzer may be
the man who knows

724
00:39:03,297 --> 00:39:05,081
the San Joaquin killer best.

725
00:39:05,168 --> 00:39:07,997
Tell us, Jim,
will he kill again?

726
00:39:08,084 --> 00:39:10,348
Well, Karen,
I hate to say this

727
00:39:10,435 --> 00:39:13,176
but, yes, I-I think this man
will strike again

728
00:39:13,263 --> 00:39:16,963
and I think that there will be
many, many more victims.

729
00:39:17,050 --> 00:39:18,530
A dark prediction.

730
00:39:18,617 --> 00:39:20,575
Uh, what makes you
think that?

731
00:39:20,662 --> 00:39:24,797
Well, he is growing more bold
and more confident every day.

732
00:39:24,884 --> 00:39:27,234
More sure of his abilities
and his greatness.

733
00:39:27,321 --> 00:39:28,670
'And why shouldn't he be?'

734
00:39:28,757 --> 00:39:31,456
'He's been able to evade
detection so easily.'

735
00:39:31,543 --> 00:39:33,936
He is too good to be caught.

736
00:39:35,721 --> 00:39:37,331
Patrick, thoughts?

737
00:39:43,468 --> 00:39:45,600
Patrick?

738
00:39:45,687 --> 00:39:49,169
It's funny. Red John thought
exactly the same thing.

739
00:39:49,256 --> 00:39:52,694
[scoffs]
Somehow, I don't think
the San Joaquin killer

740
00:39:52,781 --> 00:39:55,393
will be quite as easy
to kill as Red John.

741
00:39:55,480 --> 00:39:57,003
You're very much mistaken.

742
00:39:57,090 --> 00:40:00,354
Red John was an accomplished
killer, no doubt.

743
00:40:00,441 --> 00:40:03,357
And I applaud you
for ridding us of him.

744
00:40:03,444 --> 00:40:06,578
But there is no comparison
to the San Joaquin killer.

745
00:40:06,665 --> 00:40:09,537
'Red John was
a common sociopath.'

746
00:40:09,624 --> 00:40:12,061
Lazy, sloppy, delusional.

747
00:40:12,148 --> 00:40:14,107
You really have no idea
what you're saying.

748
00:40:14,194 --> 00:40:17,763
- Red John--
- Red John is dead.

749
00:40:17,850 --> 00:40:19,634
And the fact
that he allowed himself

750
00:40:19,721 --> 00:40:22,637
to be caught and killed by you
just proves my point.

751
00:40:24,639 --> 00:40:28,643
Compared to S.J.K.,
Red John was nothing.

752
00:40:28,730 --> 00:40:31,603
An amateur, already forgotten.

753
00:40:37,783 --> 00:40:39,524
[knock on door]

754
00:40:41,917 --> 00:40:43,092
You, uh..

755
00:40:45,660 --> 00:40:48,576
...you were very good
out there.

756
00:40:48,663 --> 00:40:50,796
Well...thank you.

757
00:41:00,980 --> 00:41:02,764
I wasgood.

